window.WB_BOOK=window.WB_BOOK||{};window.WB_BOOK["week11"]={"id":"week11","n":12,"section":"D","label":"1-to-1 Session 4","title":"Electromagnetic Induction & Transformers","titleText":"Electromagnetic Induction & Transformers","subtitle":"Making electricity from magnets: Faraday, Lenz, the a.c. generator, the transformer and the National Grid","intro":"Session 3 ended with the motor effect: a current in a magnetic field feels a force, so it MOVES. This session turns that idea around. If you MOVE a wire in a magnetic field, a voltage (e.m.f.) is made in the wire. This is called electromagnetic induction. It is how almost all of the world's electricity is generated, including at every power station in the Caribbean. You will learn (1) how to show induction with a magnet and a coil, (2) what makes the induced e.m.f. bigger (Faraday) and which way the current flows (Lenz and Fleming's RIGHT-hand rule), (3) how an a.c. generator works and what its voltage–time graph looks like, (4) how a transformer changes the size of an a.c. voltage, and (5) why electricity is sent across the country at very high voltage. The maths is short: two transformer equations and ⟪P=I^{2}R⟫. Most marks come from explanations with the right key words: \"cuts magnetic field lines\", \"changing magnetic field\", \"opposes the change\", \"smaller current, less heat lost\". This is the last part of Section D, so finish it strongly.","objectives":[{"code":"D7.9","text":"Conduct simple experiments to demonstrate electromagnetic induction (induced e.m.f.)"},{"code":"D7.10","text":"State and use the factors which affect the magnitude of an induced e.m.f. (Faraday's law)"},{"code":"D7.11","text":"Determine the direction of an induced current using Lenz's law and Fleming's right-hand rule"},{"code":"D7.12","text":"Describe the action of a simple a.c. generator and sketch its voltage–time graph"},{"code":"D7.13","text":"Explain the principle of operation of a transformer"},{"code":"D7.14","text":"Discuss the advantages of using a.c. for the transmission of electrical energy"},{"code":"D7.15","text":"Use the ideal transformer equations ⟪Vp/Vs=Np/Ns⟫ and VpIp = VsIs"}],"formulaCard":[{"text":"e.m.f. ∝ rate of cutting field lines","meaning":"Faraday's law: faster, stronger magnet, more turns, bigger area → bigger e.m.f.","units":"V"},{"text":"Lenz's law","meaning":"Induced current opposes the change producing it (conservation of energy)","units":"—"},{"text":"Fleming's RIGHT hand: thuMb Motion, First Field, seCond Current","meaning":"Direction of induced current in a moving wire (generators)","units":"—"},{"tex":"f=1/T","meaning":"Frequency of a.c.; simple generator: f = revolutions per second","units":"Hz, s"},{"text":"Generator e.m.f.: 0 when coil vertical, max when horizontal","meaning":"Faster rotation → bigger peak AND higher frequency","units":"V"},{"tex":"V_{p}/V_{s}=N_{p}/N_{s}","meaning":"Transformer turns-ratio equation","units":"V; turns"},{"tex":"V_{p}I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}","meaning":"Ideal (100% efficient) transformer: power in = power out","units":"W"},{"tex":"\\text{efficiency }=(V_{s}I_{s}\\div V_{p}I_{p})\\times 100\\%","meaning":"Real transformer efficiency","units":"%"},{"tex":"I=P/V","meaning":"Current in transmission line for power P sent at voltage V","units":"A"},{"tex":"P_{\\text{loss}}=I^{2}R","meaning":"Power wasted as heat in cables of resistance R","units":"W"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Electromagnetic induction","def":"Production of an e.m.f. in a conductor when it cuts magnetic field lines or the field linking it changes."},{"term":"Induced e.m.f.","def":"The voltage produced by electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Induced current","def":"The current driven by an induced e.m.f. in a complete circuit."},{"term":"Centre-zero galvanometer","def":"A sensitive meter whose needle can move either way, showing the size and direction of a small current."},{"term":"Faraday's law","def":"Induced e.m.f. is proportional to the rate of cutting of magnetic field lines."},{"term":"Lenz's law","def":"The induced current always opposes the change producing it."},{"term":"Fleming's right-hand rule","def":"Thumb = motion, first finger = field, second finger = induced current; used for generators."},{"term":"Generator","def":"A device that changes kinetic energy into electrical energy by electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Slip rings","def":"Two complete rings joined to the ends of a generator coil, keeping it connected to the external circuit (a.c. output)."},{"term":"Carbon brushes","def":"Sliding contacts that press on slip rings or a commutator."},{"term":"Alternating current (a.c.)","def":"Current that repeatedly reverses its direction."},{"term":"Frequency","def":"Number of complete cycles per second; unit hertz (Hz)."},{"term":"Mutual induction","def":"A changing current in one coil induces an e.m.f. in a nearby coil."},{"term":"Transformer","def":"A device that steps an alternating voltage up or down using electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Step-up transformer","def":"More turns on the secondary than the primary; output voltage greater than input."},{"term":"Step-down transformer","def":"Fewer turns on the secondary than the primary; output voltage less than input."},{"term":"Laminated core","def":"Core made of thin insulated iron sheets to reduce eddy currents."},{"term":"Eddy currents","def":"Small circulating currents induced in a metal core, which waste energy as heat."},{"term":"Ideal transformer","def":"A 100% efficient transformer: input power = output power."},{"term":"Transmission lines (grid)","def":"The network of high-voltage cables carrying electrical energy from power stations to consumers."}],"lessons":[{"id":"week11.11.1","kind":"unit","unit":"11.1","title":"Electromagnetic Induction: Making an e.m.f. with a Magnet","titleText":"Electromagnetic Induction: Making an e.m.f. with a Magnet","goals":[{"code":"D7.9","text":"Conduct simple experiments to demonstrate electromagnetic induction (induced e.m.f.)"},{"code":"D7.10","text":"State and use the factors which affect the magnitude of an induced e.m.f. (Faraday's law)"}],"codes":["D7.9","D7.10"],"words":[{"term":"Electromagnetic induction","def":"Production of an e.m.f. in a conductor when it cuts magnetic field lines or the field linking it changes."},{"term":"Induced e.m.f.","def":"The voltage produced by electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Induced current","def":"The current driven by an induced e.m.f. in a complete circuit."},{"term":"Centre-zero galvanometer","def":"A sensitive meter whose needle can move either way, showing the size and direction of a small current."},{"term":"Faraday's law","def":"Induced e.m.f. is proportional to the rate of cutting of magnetic field lines."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"What is electromagnetic induction?"},{"type":"p","text":"In 1831 **Michael Faraday** discovered that you can make electricity from magnetism. If a wire **moves through a magnetic field**, or a magnetic field **changes** near a coil, a voltage is produced in the wire. We say the voltage is **induced**. There is no battery: the energy comes from the movement."},{"type":"def","term":"Electromagnetic induction","text":"the production of an e.m.f. (voltage) in a conductor when it cuts magnetic field lines, or when the magnetic field linking a coil changes."},{"type":"def","term":"Induced e.m.f.","text":"the voltage produced by electromagnetic induction. If the conductor is part of a complete circuit, the induced e.m.f. drives an **induced current**."},{"type":"p","text":"Think of magnetic field lines as invisible threads. The wire must **cut** through the threads. If the wire moves **along** the threads, nothing is cut and nothing is induced."},{"type":"h","text":"Demonstration 1 — moving a wire between magnet poles"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/wire_moving","alt":"Figure 11.1 — Moving a wire through a magnetic field induces an e.m.f.","caption":"Figure 11.1 — Moving a wire through a magnetic field induces an e.m.f.","width":4.4,"w":974,"h":770},{"type":"p","text":"A straight copper wire is connected to a **centre-zero galvanometer** (a very sensitive meter whose needle can move left OR right, so it shows the direction of a small current)."},{"type":"table","headers":["What you do","What the galvanometer does","Why"],"rows":[["Move the wire DOWN through the field","Needle flicks one way (say right)","Wire cuts field lines → e.m.f. induced"],["Move the wire UP","Needle flicks the OTHER way (left)","Opposite motion → opposite current"],["Hold the wire still in the field","Reads ZERO","No field lines being cut"],["Move the wire along the field (N → S direction)","Reads ZERO","Moving parallel to the lines — none are cut"],["Move the wire faster","Bigger deflection","Field lines cut at a greater rate"]]},{"type":"h","text":"Demonstration 2 — a bar magnet and a coil"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/magnet_coil","alt":"Figure 11.2 — Pushing a magnet into a coil connected to a centre-zero galvanometer","caption":"Figure 11.2 — Pushing a magnet into a coil connected to a centre-zero galvanometer","width":4.4,"w":1063,"h":654},{"type":"table","headers":["What you do","What the galvanometer does"],"rows":[["Push the N pole INTO the coil","Needle flicks right (for example)"],["Hold the magnet STILL inside the coil","**Zero** — no change, no e.m.f."],["Pull the N pole OUT of the coil","Needle flicks LEFT (opposite way)"],["Push the S pole in","Needle flicks LEFT (opposite to N going in)"],["Push the magnet in FASTER","BIGGER deflection"],["Keep the magnet still and move the COIL instead","Same effect — only RELATIVE motion matters"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"An e.m.f. is induced **only while something is changing** (moving). A magnet sitting still inside a coil induces NOTHING, however strong it is."},{"type":"warn","text":"Writing \"the magnet produces a current\". The magnet alone does nothing. Write: \"the **moving** magnet causes the coil to **cut magnetic field lines** (the magnetic field in the coil **changes**), so an e.m.f. is **induced**.\""},{"type":"h","text":"Faraday's law — how BIG is the induced e.m.f.?"},{"type":"def","term":"Faraday's law of electromagnetic induction","text":"the size of the induced e.m.f. is directly proportional to the rate at which the conductor cuts magnetic field lines (the rate of change of the magnetic field linking the coil)."},{"type":"p","text":"\"Rate\" means **how fast**. Anything that makes more field lines get cut each second makes a bigger e.m.f."},{"type":"table","headers":["Factor","Change that INCREASES the e.m.f.","Why"],"rows":[["Speed of motion","Move the magnet (or wire) FASTER","More field lines cut per second"],["Strength of magnet","Use a STRONGER magnet","More field lines to cut"],["Number of turns on the coil","Use MORE turns","Each turn has its own e.m.f.; they add up"],["Area of coil","Use a coil with a LARGER area","Each turn cuts more lines"],["Iron core","Put a soft-iron core in the coil","Concentrates the field lines"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"For a 3-mark \"state three ways to increase the induced e.m.f.\" question, the safest three are: **move faster, stronger magnet, more turns**. Do not write \"bigger galvanometer\" or \"more batteries\" — there is no battery!"},{"type":"h","text":"Investigation: how does the number of turns affect the induced e.m.f.? (D7.9/D7.10)"},{"type":"p","text":"**Apparatus:** three coils of 50, 100 and 200 turns (same area, same wire), a bar magnet, a cardboard tube to guide the magnet, a metre rule, a centre-zero galvanometer (or a data-logger with voltage sensor)."},{"type":"steps","items":["Connect the 50-turn coil to the galvanometer. Clamp the coil with its axis vertical and the tube through it.","Hold the magnet, N pole down, a fixed height (e.g. 20 cm) above the coil, measured with the metre rule.","Release the magnet so it falls through the coil. Record the **maximum** deflection of the needle.","Repeat three times and find the average maximum deflection.","Repeat with the 100-turn and 200-turn coils."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Variables:** independent = number of turns; dependent = maximum deflection (induced e.m.f.); controlled = same magnet, same drop height (so same speed), same galvanometer, same coil area."},{"type":"p","text":"**Precautions:** drop (do not throw) the magnet from exactly the same height each time; keep the magnet away from the galvanometer; read the needle at eye level (avoid parallax); let the needle settle at zero before each drop."},{"type":"p","text":"**Expected results and conclusion:** the maximum deflection roughly doubles when the number of turns doubles. The induced e.m.f. is directly proportional to the number of turns."},{"type":"table","headers":["Number of turns","50","100","200"],"rows":[["Average maximum deflection / divisions","4","8","16"]]}],"examples":[{"id":"week11.11.1.e1","title":"Explaining a galvanometer reading","question":"A student pushes a magnet into a coil and the galvanometer needle moves. She then holds the magnet still inside the coil. State and explain what the galvanometer shows now.","steps":[{"label":"Observation","text":"The needle returns to zero."},{"label":"Reason","text":"The magnet is not moving, so the coil does not cut any magnetic field lines (the field in the coil is not changing)."},{"label":"Conclusion","text":"No e.m.f. is induced, so there is no current."}],"answer":"Zero reading — no relative motion, no field lines cut, so no induced e.m.f."},{"id":"week11.11.1.e2","title":"Using Faraday's law (proportion)","question":"A magnet pushed into a 100-turn coil at a certain speed induces 6.0 mV. What e.m.f. is induced if (a) a 300-turn coil is used at the same speed; (b) the 300-turn coil is used and the magnet moves at half the speed?","steps":[{"label":"Idea","text":"e.m.f. ∝ number of turns, and e.m.f. ∝ speed (rate of cutting)."},{"label":"(a) Turns × 3","text":"e.m.f. ⟪=6.0\\,\\mathrm{mV}\\times 3=18\\,\\mathrm{mV}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Speed ÷ 2","text":"e.m.f. ⟪=18\\,\\mathrm{mV}\\div 2=9.0\\,\\mathrm{mV}⟫"}],"answer":"(a) 18 mV  (b) 9.0 mV"},{"id":"week11.11.1.e3","title":"Which way does the needle move?","question":"Pushing the N pole into a coil makes the needle flick to the RIGHT. Predict what happens when (a) the N pole is pulled out; (b) the S pole is pushed in; (c) the S pole is pulled out.","steps":[{"label":"Rule","text":"Reversing EITHER the motion OR the pole reverses the current. Reversing BOTH gives the original direction."},{"label":"(a)","text":"motion reversed → LEFT"},{"label":"(b)","text":"pole reversed → LEFT"},{"label":"(c)","text":"pole AND motion reversed → RIGHT"}],"answer":"(a) left  (b) left  (c) right"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week11.11.1.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define **electromagnetic induction**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The production of an e.m.f./voltage in a conductor (1) when it cuts magnetic field lines / when the magnetic field linking it changes (1).","points":[{"text":"The production of an e.m.f./voltage in a conductor","marks":1},{"text":"when it cuts magnetic field lines / when the magnetic field linking it changes","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week11.11.1.p2","kind":"self","q":"Why is a **centre-zero** galvanometer used in induction experiments rather than an ordinary ammeter?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"It is very sensitive, so it detects the very small induced currents (1); its needle can move either way, so it shows the DIRECTION of the current (1).","points":[{"text":"It is very sensitive, so it detects the very small induced currents","marks":1},{"text":"its needle can move either way, so it shows the DIRECTION of the current","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week11.11.1.p3","kind":"self","q":"A magnet rests inside a coil connected to a galvanometer. What does the galvanometer read? Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Zero (1); the magnet is not moving so no field lines are cut / the field is not changing, so no e.m.f. is induced (1).","points":[{"text":"Zero","marks":1},{"text":"the magnet is not moving so no field lines are cut / the field is not changing, so no e.m.f. is induced","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explaining a galvanometer reading). Start like this: Observation: The needle returns to zero.","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.1.p4","kind":"self","q":"A wire is moved between the poles of a magnet in the same direction as the field lines (from N towards S). Is an e.m.f. induced? Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"No (1); the wire moves parallel to the field lines, so it does not cut any (1).","points":[{"text":"No","marks":1},{"text":"the wire moves parallel to the field lines, so it does not cut any","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week11.11.1.p5","kind":"self","q":"State **Faraday's law** of electromagnetic induction.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The induced e.m.f. is (directly) proportional (1) to the rate of cutting of magnetic field lines / rate of change of magnetic flux linkage (1).","points":[{"text":"The induced e.m.f. is (directly) proportional","marks":1},{"text":"to the rate of cutting of magnetic field lines / rate of change of magnetic flux linkage","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explaining a galvanometer reading). Start like this: Observation: The needle returns to zero.","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.1.p6","kind":"self","q":"State THREE ways of increasing the induced e.m.f. when a magnet is pushed into a coil.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any three (1 each): move the magnet faster; use a stronger magnet; use more turns on the coil; use a coil of larger area; put a soft-iron core in the coil.","points":[{"text":"Any three (1 each): move the magnet faster; use a stronger magnet; use more turns on the coil; use a coil of larger area; put a soft-iron core in the coil.","marks":3,"each":true}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Using Faraday's law (proportion)). Start like this: Idea: e.m.f. ∝ number of turns, and e.m.f. ∝ speed (rate of cutting).","from":2}},{"id":"week11.11.1.p7","kind":"self","q":"A magnet pushed into a 200-turn coil induces 4.0 mV. Calculate the e.m.f. when (a) a 600-turn coil is used at the same speed; (b) the 600-turn coil is used and the magnet moves twice as fast.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"(a) e.m.f. ∝ turns: ⟪4.0\\times 3⟫ (1) ⟪=12\\,\\mathrm{mV}⟫ (1). (b) e.m.f. ∝ speed: ⟪12\\times 2=24\\,\\mathrm{mV}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) e.m.f. ∝ turns: ⟪4.0\\times 3⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=12\\,\\mathrm{mV}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(b) e.m.f. ∝ speed: ⟪12\\times 2=24\\,\\mathrm{mV}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Using Faraday's law (proportion)). Start like this: Idea: e.m.f. ∝ number of turns, and e.m.f. ∝ speed (rate of cutting).","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week11.11.1.p8","kind":"self","q":"Pushing the S pole into a coil makes the needle flick LEFT. Which way will it flick when the N pole is pulled out of the coil? Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"LEFT (1): both the pole and the direction of motion are reversed, so the two reversals cancel (1).","points":[{"text":"LEFT","marks":1},{"text":": both the pole and the direction of motion are reversed, so the two reversals cancel","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week11.11.1.p9","kind":"self","q":"Plan an experiment to show how the speed of the magnet affects the induced e.m.f. Name the independent, dependent and TWO controlled variables, and give ONE precaution.","marks":5,"level":3,"lines":7,"scheme":"Drop magnet through coil from different heights (greater height → faster) / push at different speeds, record maximum galvanometer deflection (1). Independent: speed (drop height) (1); dependent: maximum deflection/e.m.f. (1); controlled: same magnet, same coil/number of turns (1); precaution: repeat and average / read at eye level / magnet released not thrown / allow needle to return to zero (1).","points":[{"text":"Drop magnet through coil from different heights (greater height → faster) / push at different speeds, record maximum galvanometer deflection","marks":1},{"text":"Independent: speed (drop height)","marks":1},{"text":"dependent: maximum deflection/e.m.f.","marks":1},{"text":"controlled: same magnet, same coil/number of turns","marks":1},{"text":"precaution: repeat and average / read at eye level / magnet released not thrown / allow needle to return to zero","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week11.11.1.w1","kind":"self","q":"A 240 V kettle is rated 2400 W. What fuse (3 A, 5 A or 13 A) should it have?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪I=2400\\div 240=10\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫, so 13 A.","points":[{"text":"⟪I=2400\\div 240=10\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫, so 13 A.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week10.r1"},{"id":"week11.11.1.w2","kind":"self","q":"What does the earth wire do when a live wire touches a metal case?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"It carries a large current to the ground, which blows the fuse and cuts off the live supply.","points":[{"text":"It carries a large current to the ground, which blows the fuse and cuts off the live supply.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week10.r2"},{"id":"week11.11.1.w3","kind":"self","q":"Sketch (in words) the output of a half-wave rectifier.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Positive humps with flat zero gaps between them (the negative halves are removed).","points":[{"text":"Positive humps with flat zero gaps between them (the negative halves are removed).","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week10.r3"},{"id":"week11.11.1.w4","kind":"short","q":"Which gate gives 1 only when both inputs are 0?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"NOR.","points":[{"text":"NOR.","marks":1}],"accept":["NOR"],"section":"warmup","src":"week10.r4"},{"id":"week11.11.1.w5","kind":"self","q":"In Fleming's left-hand rule, what do the first finger, second finger and thumb represent?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Field (N → S), current, and motion/force.","points":[{"text":"Field (N → S), current, and motion/force.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week10.r5"}],"quickStart":[{"type":"p","text":"This session builds directly on Sessions 2 and 3. Check these five facts before Unit 11.1."},{"type":"table","headers":["Idea","What you must remember"],"rows":[["Magnetic field lines","Go from **N to S** outside a magnet. Closer lines = stronger field."],["Right-hand grip rule","Grip a coil with your RIGHT hand, fingers curling the way the current flows: your thumb points to the **N pole** of the coil."],["Fleming's LEFT-hand rule (motors)","thuMb = Motion (force), First finger = Field, seCond finger = Current."],["d.c. and a.c.","d.c. flows one way only (cell, battery). a.c. keeps reversing direction (mains)."],["Electrical power","⟪P=IV⟫ (watts). Also ⟪P=I^{2}R⟫, because ⟪V=IR⟫."]]},{"type":"remember","text":"Motor effect: current + magnetic field → MOTION. Induction (this session) is the reverse: motion + magnetic field → CURRENT. Same parts, opposite job."},{"type":"fill","text":"Quick check: a heater takes 5.0 A and has resistance 48 Ω. Use ⟪P=I^{2}R⟫ to find its power.   P = ","lines":1},{"type":"fill","text":"Quick check: which pole is at the end of a coil if, looking at that end, the current flows anticlockwise?","lines":1}],"n":86},{"id":"week11.11.2","kind":"unit","unit":"11.2","title":"Which Way? Lenz's Law and Fleming's Right-Hand Rule","titleText":"Which Way? Lenz's Law and Fleming's Right-Hand Rule","goals":[{"code":"D7.11","text":"Determine the direction of an induced current using Lenz's law and Fleming's right-hand rule"}],"codes":["D7.11"],"words":[{"term":"Induced current","def":"The current driven by an induced e.m.f. in a complete circuit."},{"term":"Lenz's law","def":"The induced current always opposes the change producing it."},{"term":"Fleming's right-hand rule","def":"Thumb = motion, first finger = field, second finger = induced current; used for generators."},{"term":"Generator","def":"A device that changes kinetic energy into electrical energy by electromagnetic induction."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Lenz's law"},{"type":"def","term":"Lenz's law","text":"the direction of the induced current is always such that it OPPOSES the change (the motion) that produces it."},{"type":"p","text":"In simple words: the coil **fights back**. Whatever you do to the coil, the induced current makes a magnetic field that tries to stop you."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/lenz","alt":"Figure 11.3 — Lenz's law: (a) the coil repels an approaching N pole; (b) the coil attracts a departing N pole","caption":"Figure 11.3 — Lenz's law: (a) the coil repels an approaching N pole; (b) the coil attracts a departing N pole","width":5.4,"w":1209,"h":680},{"type":"steps","items":["(a) You push a **N pole towards** the coil.","To oppose this, the induced current makes the near end of the coil a **N pole**.","N repels N, so you must PUSH to move the magnet in.","(b) You pull the **N pole away** from the coil.","To oppose this, the near end of the coil becomes a **S pole**.","S attracts N, so you must PULL to remove the magnet."]},{"type":"h","text":"Why must it oppose? — conservation of energy"},{"type":"p","text":"The electrical energy in the coil must come from somewhere. Because the coil **opposes** the motion, you have to **do work** (push or pull against a force). That work is changed into electrical energy. If the induced current HELPED the motion, the magnet would speed up by itself and make electricity for free — energy would be created from nothing, which is impossible."},{"type":"remember","text":"Lenz's law is just the **principle of conservation of energy** applied to induction. Work done pushing the magnet → electrical energy in the coil."},{"type":"h","text":"Finding the current direction in the coil"},{"type":"p","text":"Once you know which pole the coil end must become, use the **right-hand grip rule** (Session 3), or this end-view trick:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Looking at the end of the coil, the current flows…","That end is a…"],"rows":[["ANTICLOCKWISE","**N pole**"],["CLOCKWISE","**S pole**"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Memory aid: draw the letters. An **N** with arrows on its ends goes anticlockwise; an **S** goes clockwise."},{"type":"h","text":"Fleming's right-hand rule (for generators)"},{"type":"p","text":"For a straight **wire** moving through a field, use Fleming's **RIGHT**-hand rule. Hold the thumb and first two fingers of your RIGHT hand all at right angles to each other."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/fleming_rhr","alt":"Figure 11.4 — Fleming's right-hand rule","caption":"Figure 11.4 — Fleming's right-hand rule","width":3.4,"w":683,"h":527},{"type":"table","headers":["Finger","Stands for","Points…"],"rows":[["Thu**M**b","**M**otion of the wire","the way the wire is moved"],["**F**irst finger","**F**ield","from N to S"],["Se**C**ond finger","induced **C**urrent","conventional current (+ to −)"]]},{"type":"table","headers":["","Fleming's LEFT-hand rule","Fleming's RIGHT-hand rule"],"rows":[["Used for","MOTORS (motor effect)","GENERATORS (induction)"],["You are given","field + current","field + motion"],["You find","direction of the FORCE / motion","direction of the INDUCED CURRENT"],["Energy change","electrical → kinetic","kinetic → electrical"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"Memory aid: gene-RIGHT-or. Generators use the RIGHT hand. Motors use the left hand (\"driving on the left\" — Caribbean traffic drives on the left!)."},{"type":"warn","text":"Using the wrong hand. In an exam, read the question: if the wire is MOVED by you (or a turbine) and you need the CURRENT, it is the RIGHT hand."},{"type":"p","text":"**Symbols for wires in the page:** ⊗ (cross) = current going INTO the page; ⊙ (dot) = current coming OUT of the page."}],"examples":[{"id":"week11.11.2.e1","title":"Lenz's law with a coil","question":"The N pole of a magnet is pushed into the left end of a coil. (a) What pole is produced at the left end of the coil? (b) Looking at the left end from the magnet, which way does the induced current flow?","steps":[{"label":"Lenz","text":"the coil must oppose the N pole coming in, so it must REPEL it"},{"label":"(a)","text":"N repels N → the left end becomes a N pole"},{"label":"(b)","text":"a N pole seen end-on has ANTICLOCKWISE current"}],"answer":"(a) North pole  (b) anticlockwise (as seen from the magnet)"},{"id":"week11.11.2.e2","title":"Fleming's right-hand rule","question":"A wire lies between a N pole on the LEFT and a S pole on the RIGHT, running into the page. The wire is moved UPWARDS. Find the direction of the induced current.","steps":[{"label":"First finger (Field)","text":"points from N to S → to the RIGHT"},{"label":"Thumb (Motion)","text":"points UP"},{"label":"Second finger (Current)","text":"with the right hand in this position, the second finger points INTO the page"}],"answer":"The induced current flows INTO the page (⊗)."},{"id":"week11.11.2.e3","title":"Energy and Lenz's law","question":"Explain why it is harder to push a magnet into a coil when the coil is connected in a complete circuit than when the coil is disconnected.","steps":[{"label":"Complete circuit","text":"an induced current flows; by Lenz's law it makes the coil end repel the magnet"},{"label":"Work","text":"you must do extra work against this force; that work becomes electrical energy (heat in the coil)"},{"label":"Disconnected","text":"an e.m.f. is induced but no current flows, so there is no magnetic field to oppose you"}],"answer":"With a complete circuit, the induced current opposes the motion (Lenz), so work must be done against the repulsion; with no current there is no opposing force."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week11.11.2.p1","kind":"self","q":"State **Lenz's law**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The direction of the induced current/e.m.f. (1) is such that it opposes the change (motion) producing it (1).","points":[{"text":"The direction of the induced current/e.m.f.","marks":1},{"text":"is such that it opposes the change (motion) producing it","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Lenz's law with a coil). Start like this: Lenz: the coil must oppose the N pole coming in, so it must REPEL it","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.2.p2","kind":"self","q":"The N pole of a magnet is moved towards one end of a coil. State the polarity of that end of the coil and give a reason.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"North pole (1); to repel / oppose the approaching N pole (Lenz's law) (1).","points":[{"text":"North pole","marks":1},{"text":"to repel / oppose the approaching N pole (Lenz's law)","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Lenz's law with a coil). Start like this: Lenz: the coil must oppose the N pole coming in, so it must REPEL it","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week11.11.2.p3","kind":"self","q":"The S pole of a magnet is pulled AWAY from one end of a coil. State the polarity of that end of the coil.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"North pole (1) — it attracts the departing S pole, opposing its removal.","points":[{"text":"North pole. — it attracts the departing S pole, opposing its removal","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Lenz's law with a coil). Start like this: Lenz: the coil must oppose the N pole coming in, so it must REPEL it","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.2.p4","kind":"self","q":"Explain how Lenz's law is an example of the principle of conservation of energy.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"The induced current opposes the motion (1), so work must be done to move the magnet/coil (1); this work done is converted into electrical energy — energy is not created from nothing (1).","points":[{"text":"The induced current opposes the motion","marks":1},{"text":"so work must be done to move the magnet/coil","marks":1},{"text":"this work done is converted into electrical energy — energy is not created from nothing","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Energy and Lenz's law). Start like this: Complete circuit: an induced current flows; by Lenz's law it makes the coil end repel the magnet","from":3}},{"id":"week11.11.2.p5","kind":"self","q":"In Fleming's right-hand rule, state what is represented by (a) the thumb, (b) the first finger, (c) the second finger.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) motion of the conductor (1) (b) magnetic field, N to S (1) (c) induced (conventional) current (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) motion of the conductor","marks":1},{"text":"(b) magnetic field, N to S","marks":1},{"text":"(c) induced (conventional) current","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Fleming's right-hand rule). Start like this: First finger (Field): points from N to S → to the RIGHT","from":2}},{"id":"week11.11.2.p6","kind":"self","q":"A wire runs into the page between a N pole on the left and a S pole on the right. The wire is moved DOWNWARDS. Use Fleming's right-hand rule to find the direction of the induced current.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Field right, motion down (1) → current OUT of the page (⊙) (1).","points":[{"text":"Field right, motion down","marks":1},{"text":"→ current OUT of the page (⊙)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Fleming's right-hand rule). Start like this: First finger (Field): points from N to S → to the RIGHT","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week11.11.2.p7","kind":"self","q":"Looking at the end of a coil, a N pole of a magnet approaches. Which way (clockwise or anticlockwise) does the induced current flow in that end, as seen by the magnet?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"That end becomes N to repel the magnet (1), so the current is anticlockwise (1).","points":[{"text":"That end becomes N to repel the magnet","marks":1},{"text":"so the current is anticlockwise","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week11.11.2.p8","kind":"self","q":"A small strong magnet is dropped down a vertical copper tube and then down a plastic tube of the same size. It takes much longer to fall through the copper tube. Explain.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"The falling magnet induces currents in the copper (a conductor) (1); by Lenz's law these currents produce a magnetic field that opposes the motion of the magnet / slows it (1); plastic is an insulator, so no induced current and no opposing force (1).","points":[{"text":"The falling magnet induces currents in the copper (a conductor)","marks":1},{"text":"by Lenz's law these currents produce a magnetic field that opposes the motion of the magnet / slows it","marks":1},{"text":"plastic is an insulator, so no induced current and no opposing force","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week11.11.2.w1","kind":"self","q":"A magnet rests inside a coil connected to a galvanometer. What does the galvanometer read? Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Zero (1); the magnet is not moving so no field lines are cut / the field is not changing, so no e.m.f. is induced (1).","points":[{"text":"Zero","marks":1},{"text":"the magnet is not moving so no field lines are cut / the field is not changing, so no e.m.f. is induced","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.1.p3"},{"id":"week11.11.2.w2","kind":"self","q":"Define **electromagnetic induction**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The production of an e.m.f./voltage in a conductor (1) when it cuts magnetic field lines / when the magnetic field linking it changes (1).","points":[{"text":"The production of an e.m.f./voltage in a conductor","marks":1},{"text":"when it cuts magnetic field lines / when the magnetic field linking it changes","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.1.p1"},{"id":"week11.11.2.w3","kind":"self","q":"A wire is moved between the poles of a magnet in the same direction as the field lines (from N towards S). Is an e.m.f. induced? Explain.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"No (1); the wire moves parallel to the field lines, so it does not cut any (1).","points":[{"text":"No","marks":1},{"text":"the wire moves parallel to the field lines, so it does not cut any","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.1.p4"}],"n":87},{"id":"week11.11.3","kind":"unit","unit":"11.3","title":"The a.c. Generator","titleText":"The a.c. Generator","goals":[{"code":"D7.12","text":"Describe the action of a simple a.c. generator and sketch its voltage–time graph"}],"codes":["D7.12"],"words":[{"term":"Electromagnetic induction","def":"Production of an e.m.f. in a conductor when it cuts magnetic field lines or the field linking it changes."},{"term":"Induced e.m.f.","def":"The voltage produced by electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Fleming's right-hand rule","def":"Thumb = motion, first finger = field, second finger = induced current; used for generators."},{"term":"Generator","def":"A device that changes kinetic energy into electrical energy by electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Slip rings","def":"Two complete rings joined to the ends of a generator coil, keeping it connected to the external circuit (a.c. output)."},{"term":"Carbon brushes","def":"Sliding contacts that press on slip rings or a commutator."},{"term":"Frequency","def":"Number of complete cycles per second; unit hertz (Hz)."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"A **generator** (dynamo/alternator) changes **kinetic energy** into **electrical energy** by electromagnetic induction. In a power station, a turbine (turned by steam, water or wind) spins a coil (or a magnet) very fast."},{"type":"h","text":"Parts of a simple a.c. generator"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/ac_generator","alt":"Figure 11.5 — A simple a.c. generator","caption":"Figure 11.5 — A simple a.c. generator","width":5.2,"w":1300,"h":854},{"type":"table","headers":["Part","What it does"],"rows":[["Coil (armature)","Many turns of wire on an axle. Rotated in the field so its sides cut field lines."],["Magnets (N and S poles)","Provide the magnetic field."],["Slip rings","Two **separate, complete** rings, each joined to ONE end of the coil. They turn with the coil, so each end of the coil is always connected to the same brush and the wires do not twist."],["Carbon brushes","Press against the slip rings and carry the current to the outside circuit (load)."]]},{"type":"h","text":"How it works"},{"type":"steps","items":["The coil is turned in the magnetic field. Its two long sides **cut magnetic field lines**, so an **e.m.f. is induced** (Faraday).","The two sides move in opposite directions (one up, one down), so their e.m.f.s act in the same direction around the coil and add up.","After half a turn, each side has swapped places: the side that was moving UP is now moving DOWN. So the induced e.m.f. **reverses direction** (Fleming's right-hand rule).","This happens every half turn, so the output is an **alternating** e.m.f. (a.c.).","The slip rings keep each end of the coil connected to the same brush, so the reversing e.m.f. passes to the outside circuit as a.c."]},{"type":"h","text":"The voltage–time graph"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/generator_vt","alt":"Figure 11.6 — e.m.f. against time for ONE revolution of the coil","caption":"Figure 11.6 — e.m.f. against time for ONE revolution of the coil","width":5.6,"w":1254,"h":614},{"type":"table","headers":["Coil position","Sides of coil are moving…","Rate of cutting field lines","e.m.f."],"rows":[["Plane of coil VERTICAL (perpendicular to field) — 0°, 180°, 360°","PARALLEL to the field lines (straight up/down along them)","zero","**ZERO**"],["Plane of coil HORIZONTAL (parallel to field) — 90°, 270°","at RIGHT ANGLES to the field lines","greatest","**MAXIMUM** (+ at 90°, − at 270°)"]]},{"type":"warn","text":"Thinking the e.m.f. is biggest when the coil faces the magnets (vertical). It is the OPPOSITE: when the coil is vertical its sides slide along the field lines and cut none, so ⟪e=0⟫. The e.m.f. is biggest when the coil is HORIZONTAL (in line with the field)."},{"type":"h","text":"Changing the output"},{"type":"table","headers":["Change","Effect on peak e.m.f.","Effect on frequency"],"rows":[["Turn the coil FASTER (e.g. twice as fast)","Increases (doubles)","Increases (doubles)"],["Stronger magnets","Increases","No change"],["More turns on the coil","Increases","No change"],["Larger coil area / soft-iron core","Increases","No change"]]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/generator_faster","alt":"Figure 11.7 — Turning the coil twice as fast doubles the peak e.m.f. AND the frequency","caption":"Figure 11.7 — Turning the coil twice as fast doubles the peak e.m.f. AND the frequency","width":5,"w":1174,"h":574},{"type":"formula","tex":"f=1/T","where":["⟪f⟫ = frequency in hertz (Hz) = number of complete cycles (revolutions of a simple generator) per second","⟪T⟫ = period in seconds (s) = time for one cycle","Mains in the Caribbean is 50 Hz or 60 Hz, depending on the country."],"label":"FREQUENCY AND PERIOD"},{"type":"tip","text":"To read a V–t graph: **peak voltage** = highest point above zero; **period T** = time for one full wave (up, down, back to start); then ⟪f=1\\div T⟫. Convert ms to s first ⟪(20\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.020\\,\\mathrm{s})⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"Generator vs motor"},{"type":"table","headers":["","d.c. MOTOR (Session 3)","a.c. GENERATOR"],"rows":[["Energy change","electrical → kinetic","kinetic → electrical"],["Connection to coil","split-ring commutator","two slip rings"],["Rule for direction","Fleming's LEFT-hand rule","Fleming's RIGHT-hand rule"],["Input / output","d.c. supply IN, rotation OUT","rotation IN, a.c. OUT"]]}],"examples":[{"id":"week11.11.3.e1","title":"Reading a generator graph","question":"The output of a generator has a peak voltage of 12 V and one complete cycle takes 20 ms. Find the frequency.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪f=1/T⟫"},{"label":"Convert","text":"⟪T=20\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.020\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪f=1\\div 0.020⟫"}],"answer":"⟪f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (peak voltage 12 V)"},{"id":"week11.11.3.e2","title":"Revolutions per minute to frequency","question":"A simple generator coil turns at 3000 revolutions per minute. Find (a) the frequency of the a.c.; (b) the period.","steps":[{"label":"(a) per second","text":"⟪3000\\div 60=50⟫ revolutions per second → one cycle per revolution → ⟪f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Formula","text":"⟪T=1/f=1\\div 50⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪T=0.020\\,\\mathrm{s}\\ (20\\,\\mathrm{ms})⟫"}],"answer":"(a) 50 Hz  (b) 0.020 s"},{"id":"week11.11.3.e3","title":"Speeding up the generator","question":"A generator gives a peak e.m.f. of 6.0 V at a frequency of 25 Hz. The coil is now turned twice as fast. State the new peak e.m.f. and the new frequency and period.","steps":[{"label":"Faster","text":"twice the rate of cutting → peak e.m.f. doubles: ⟪2\\times 6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫"},{"label":"Frequency","text":"twice as many turns each second: ⟪2\\times 25=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫"},{"label":"Period","text":"⟪T=1/50=0.020\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"}],"answer":"12 V peak, 50 Hz, period 0.020 s (Figure 11.7)"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week11.11.3.p1","kind":"self","q":"State the energy change that takes place in a generator.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Kinetic (mechanical) energy → electrical energy (1).","points":[{"text":"Kinetic (mechanical) energy → electrical energy","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a generator graph). Start like this: Formula: ⟪f=1/T⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.3.p2","kind":"self","q":"State the function of (a) the slip rings, (b) the carbon brushes in an a.c. generator.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) Keep each end of the rotating coil connected to the same brush/outside circuit without twisting the wires (1). (b) Make sliding contact with the slip rings to carry current to the external circuit (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Keep each end of the rotating coil connected to the same brush/outside circuit without twisting the wires","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Make sliding contact with the slip rings to carry current to the external circuit","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Speeding up the generator). Start like this: Faster: twice the rate of cutting → peak e.m.f. doubles: ⟪2\\times 6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫","from":3}}],"practice":[{"id":"week11.11.3.p3","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the e.m.f. of a generator is ZERO when the plane of the coil is vertical (perpendicular to the field).","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The sides of the coil move parallel to the field lines (1), so they do not cut any field lines and no e.m.f. is induced (1).","points":[{"text":"The sides of the coil move parallel to the field lines","marks":1},{"text":"so they do not cut any field lines and no e.m.f. is induced","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week11.11.3.p4","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the output of the generator is alternating.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Every half turn, each side of the coil changes from moving up to moving down through the field (1), so the direction of the induced e.m.f./current reverses (1).","points":[{"text":"Every half turn, each side of the coil changes from moving up to moving down through the field","marks":1},{"text":"so the direction of the induced e.m.f./current reverses","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a generator graph). Start like this: Formula: ⟪f=1/T⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.3.p5","kind":"num","q":"A generator's output has a peak of 15 V and a period of 40 ms. Calculate its frequency.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪T=0.040\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1); ⟪f=1/T=1/0.040⟫ (1) ⟪=25\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪T=0.040\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪f=1/T=1/0.040⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=25\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":25,"sf":2,"unit":"Hz","unitTex":"\\mathrm{Hz}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a generator graph). Start like this: Formula: ⟪f=1/T⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.3.p6","kind":"self","q":"A coil turns 60 times per second. State the frequency of the a.c. and calculate the period.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪f=60\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1); ⟪T=1/60=0.017\\,\\mathrm{s}\\ (0.0167\\,\\mathrm{s})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪f=60\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪T=1/60=0.017\\,\\mathrm{s}\\ (0.0167\\,\\mathrm{s})⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Revolutions per minute to frequency). Start like this: (a) per second: ⟪3000\\div 60=50⟫ revolutions per second → one cycle per revolution → ⟪f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week11.11.3.p7","kind":"self","q":"The speed of rotation of a generator is HALVED. State what happens to (a) the peak e.m.f., (b) the frequency.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) halved (1) (b) halved (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) halved","marks":1},{"text":"(b) halved","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Speeding up the generator). Start like this: Faster: twice the rate of cutting → peak e.m.f. doubles: ⟪2\\times 6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week11.11.3.p8","kind":"self","q":"State THREE ways to increase the peak e.m.f. of a generator WITHOUT changing its frequency.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any three (1 each): stronger magnets; more turns on the coil; larger area of coil; soft-iron core in the coil.","points":[{"text":"Any three (1 each): stronger magnets; more turns on the coil; larger area of coil; soft-iron core in the coil.","marks":3,"each":true}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Speeding up the generator). Start like this: Faster: twice the rate of cutting → peak e.m.f. doubles: ⟪2\\times 6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫","from":3}}],"exit":[{"id":"week11.11.3.p9","kind":"self","q":"Give TWO differences between an a.c. generator and a d.c. motor.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two (1 each): generator uses slip rings, motor uses a split-ring commutator; generator changes kinetic → electrical, motor electrical → kinetic; generator uses Fleming's right-hand rule, motor left-hand rule.","points":[{"text":"Any two (1 each): generator uses slip rings, motor uses a split-ring commutator; generator changes kinetic → electrical, motor electrical → kinetic; generator uses Fleming's right-hand rule, motor left-hand rule.","marks":2,"each":true}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week11.11.3.p10","kind":"self","q":"Sketch the e.m.f.–time graph for TWO complete revolutions of a generator coil. Mark with X each point where the coil is horizontal (parallel to the field).","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"Sine-shaped curve starting at zero, two complete cycles (1); peaks equal in size above and below the axis (1); X at each peak/trough — four points, at ¼, ¾, 1¼, 1¾ revolutions (1).","points":[{"text":"Sine-shaped curve starting at zero, two complete cycles","marks":1},{"text":"peaks equal in size above and below the axis","marks":1},{"text":"X at each peak/trough — four points, at ¼, ¾, 1¼, 1¾ revolutions","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week11.11.3.w1","kind":"self","q":"A wire runs into the page between a N pole on the left and a S pole on the right. The wire is moved DOWNWARDS. Use Fleming's right-hand rule to find the direction of the induced current.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Field right, motion down (1) → current OUT of the page (⊙) (1).","points":[{"text":"Field right, motion down","marks":1},{"text":"→ current OUT of the page (⊙)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.2.p6"},{"id":"week11.11.3.w2","kind":"self","q":"The N pole of a magnet is moved towards one end of a coil. State the polarity of that end of the coil and give a reason.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"North pole (1); to repel / oppose the approaching N pole (Lenz's law) (1).","points":[{"text":"North pole","marks":1},{"text":"to repel / oppose the approaching N pole (Lenz's law)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.2.p2"},{"id":"week11.11.3.w3","kind":"self","q":"The S pole of a magnet is pulled AWAY from one end of a coil. State the polarity of that end of the coil.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"North pole (1) — it attracts the departing S pole, opposing its removal.","points":[{"text":"North pole. — it attracts the departing S pole, opposing its removal","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.2.p3"}],"n":88},{"id":"week11.11.4","kind":"unit","unit":"11.4","title":"The Transformer","titleText":"The Transformer","goals":[{"code":"D7.13","text":"Explain the principle of operation of a transformer"},{"code":"D7.15","text":"Use the ideal transformer equations ⟪Vp/Vs=Np/Ns⟫ and VpIp = VsIs"}],"codes":["D7.13","D7.15"],"words":[{"term":"Electromagnetic induction","def":"Production of an e.m.f. in a conductor when it cuts magnetic field lines or the field linking it changes."},{"term":"Mutual induction","def":"A changing current in one coil induces an e.m.f. in a nearby coil."},{"term":"Transformer","def":"A device that steps an alternating voltage up or down using electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Step-up transformer","def":"More turns on the secondary than the primary; output voltage greater than input."},{"term":"Step-down transformer","def":"Fewer turns on the secondary than the primary; output voltage less than input."},{"term":"Eddy currents","def":"Small circulating currents induced in a metal core, which waste energy as heat."},{"term":"Ideal transformer","def":"A 100% efficient transformer: input power = output power."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Mutual induction — a changing current in one coil induces an e.m.f. in another"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/two_coils","alt":"Figure 11.8 — Two coils on one iron bar: the primary is connected to a cell and switch, the secondary to a galvanometer","caption":"Figure 11.8 — Two coils on one iron bar: the primary is connected to a cell and switch, the secondary to a galvanometer","width":4.8,"w":1174,"h":574},{"type":"table","headers":["What you do","Galvanometer on the secondary coil","Why"],"rows":[["Close the switch","Needle FLICKS one way, then returns to zero","Current in the primary grows → its magnetic field grows → field through secondary CHANGES → e.m.f. induced"],["Keep the switch closed","ZERO","Steady current → steady field → no change → no e.m.f."],["Open the switch","Needle flicks the OTHER way","Field collapses (changes the other way)"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"Only a **CHANGING** magnetic field induces an e.m.f. A steady d.c. current makes a steady field, which induces nothing (except for a flick at switch-on and switch-off). So we feed the primary with **a.c.**, which changes all the time."},{"type":"h","text":"Structure of a transformer"},{"type":"def","term":"Transformer","text":"a device that uses electromagnetic induction to change (step up or step down) the size of an ALTERNATING voltage."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/transformer","alt":"Figure 11.9 — A step-down transformer and its circuit symbol","caption":"Figure 11.9 — A step-down transformer and its circuit symbol","width":5.8,"w":1366,"h":617},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Primary coil** — connected to the a.c. input voltage V~p~. It has N~p~ turns.","**Secondary coil** — connected to the output (load). It has N~s~ turns and gives the output voltage V~s~.","**Soft-iron core** — links the two coils. Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised, so it concentrates the changing magnetic field and carries it through the secondary coil. It is **laminated** (made of thin sheets insulated from each other).","The two coils are **NOT** electrically connected. Energy passes from one to the other through the changing magnetic field."]},{"type":"h","text":"How a transformer works (D7.13) — learn these steps"},{"type":"steps","items":["An **alternating current** flows in the primary coil.","This produces an **alternating (constantly changing) magnetic field** in the soft-iron core.","The core carries this changing field through the **secondary coil**.","The changing magnetic field linking the secondary coil **induces an alternating e.m.f.** in it (electromagnetic induction).","The size of the output voltage depends on the **ratio of the turns**: more turns on the secondary → bigger voltage."]},{"type":"tip","text":"A 4-mark \"explain how a transformer works\" answer needs the key words: **alternating current** in primary → **changing magnetic field** in **core** → field **links the secondary** → **e.m.f. induced** in secondary."},{"type":"h","text":"The turns-ratio equation (D7.15)"},{"type":"formula","tex":"V_{p}/V_{s}=N_{p}/N_{s}","where":["⟪V_{p}⟫ = primary (input) voltage (V);  V~s~ = secondary (output) voltage (V)","⟪N_{p}⟫ = number of turns on primary;  N~s~ = number of turns on secondary","Rearranged:  ⟪V_{s}=V_{p}\\times N_{s}/N_{p}⟫    and    ⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}/V_{p}⟫"],"label":"TRANSFORMER EQUATION"},{"type":"table","headers":["Type","Turns","Voltage","Current","Example"],"rows":[["**Step-UP**","N~s~ > N~p~","V~s~ > V~p~ (voltage goes UP)","I~s~ < I~p~ (current goes DOWN)","Power station → transmission lines"],["**Step-DOWN**","N~s~ < N~p~","V~s~ < V~p~ (voltage goes DOWN)","I~s~ > I~p~ (current goes UP)","Phone charger, doorbell, substation → homes"]]},{"type":"h","text":"The ideal transformer: power in = power out"},{"type":"p","text":"An **ideal** transformer is 100% efficient: no energy is wasted. Power = voltage × current, so:"},{"type":"formula","tex":"V_{p}I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}","where":["⟪I_{p}⟫ = primary current (A);  I~s~ = secondary current (A)","Input power (V~p~I~p~) = output power (V~s~I~s~), in watts","If the voltage is stepped UP, the current is stepped DOWN by the same factor."],"label":"IDEAL TRANSFORMER (100% EFFICIENT)"},{"type":"warn","text":"Thinking a step-up transformer gives you \"free\" energy. It increases VOLTAGE but DECREASES current by the same factor. The power can never go up."},{"type":"formula","tex":"\\text{efficiency }=(\\text{output power }\\div \\text{ input power})\\times 100\\%=(V_{s}I_{s}\\div V_{p}I_{p})\\times 100\\%","where":["Real transformers are about 90–99% efficient."],"label":"EFFICIENCY"},{"type":"h","text":"Why real transformers waste a little energy (and how designers reduce it)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Energy loss","Cause","How it is reduced"],"rows":[["Heating of the coils","Current in the resistance of the wires (I^2^R)","Use thick copper wire (low resistance)"],["Eddy currents","The changing field induces small circulating currents in the iron core itself, which heat it","**Laminate** the core: thin iron sheets insulated from each other block the eddy currents"],["Magnetising losses (hysteresis)","Energy used to magnetise and demagnetise the core 50–60 times a second","Use **soft** iron, which is easy to magnetise and demagnetise"],["Field leakage","Some field lines from the primary miss the secondary","Good core design; wind the secondary on top of the primary"]]}],"examples":[{"id":"week11.11.4.e1","title":"Finding the number of turns","question":"A step-down transformer changes 240 V mains to 12 V for a lamp. The primary has 2000 turns. How many turns are on the secondary?","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}/V_{p}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪N_{s}=2000\\times 12/240⟫"},{"label":"Calculate","text":"⟪N_{s}=24\\,000/240=100⟫"}],"answer":"⟪N_{s}=100⟫ turns"},{"id":"week11.11.4.e2","title":"Finding the output voltage","question":"A transformer has 500 turns on the primary and 2500 turns on the secondary. The input is 110 V a.c. Find the output voltage. Is it step-up or step-down?","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪V_{s}=V_{p}\\times N_{s}/N_{p}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪V_{s}=110\\times 2500/500=110\\times 5⟫"},{"label":"Type","text":"N~s~ > N~p~ → step-up"}],"answer":"⟪V_{s}=550\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫; step-up transformer"},{"id":"week11.11.4.e3","title":"Currents in an ideal transformer","question":"A phone charger contains an ideal transformer. Input 240 V; output 6.0 V at 2.0 A. Find the current taken from the mains.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪V_{p}I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}\\rightarrow I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}/V_{p}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪I_{p}=6.0\\times 2.0/240=12/240⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪I_{p}=0.050\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫"}],"answer":"⟪I_{p}=0.050\\,\\mathrm{A}\\ (50\\,\\mathrm{mA})⟫. Power in = power out ⟪=12\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫."},{"id":"week11.11.4.e4","title":"Efficiency of a real transformer","question":"A transformer takes 0.50 A from a 240 V supply. Its output is 12 V and 9.0 A. Calculate its efficiency.","steps":[{"label":"Input power","text":"⟪V_{p}I_{p}=240\\times 0.50=120\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫"},{"label":"Output power","text":"⟪V_{s}I_{s}=12\\times 9.0=108\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫"},{"label":"Efficiency","text":"⟪(108\\div 120)\\times 100\\%=90\\%⟫"}],"answer":"Efficiency = 90% (12 W wasted, mainly as heat)"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week11.11.4.p1","kind":"self","q":"Explain why a transformer does NOT work when the primary is connected to a steady d.c. supply.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Steady d.c. gives a steady/unchanging magnetic field in the core (1), so no e.m.f. is induced in the secondary (only a brief one at switch-on/off) (1).","points":[{"text":"Steady d.c. gives a steady/unchanging magnetic field in the core","marks":1},{"text":"so no e.m.f. is induced in the secondary (only a brief one at switch-on/off)","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 4 (Efficiency of a real transformer). Start like this: Input power: ⟪V_{p}I_{p}=240\\times 0.50=120\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫","from":4}},{"id":"week11.11.4.p2","kind":"self","q":"Two coils are wound on an iron bar (Figure 11.8). State what the galvanometer shows when the switch is (a) closed, (b) kept closed, (c) opened.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) brief flick/deflection one way (1) (b) zero (1) (c) brief flick in the opposite direction (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) brief flick/deflection one way","marks":1},{"text":"(b) zero","marks":1},{"text":"(c) brief flick in the opposite direction","marks":1}],"section":"try"}],"practice":[{"id":"week11.11.4.p3","kind":"self","q":"Explain how a transformer produces a voltage in the secondary coil.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"Alternating current in primary (1) produces a changing/alternating magnetic field in the core (1); the core links the field to the secondary coil (1); the changing field induces an (alternating) e.m.f. in the secondary (1).","points":[{"text":"Alternating current in primary","marks":1},{"text":"produces a changing/alternating magnetic field in the core","marks":1},{"text":"the core links the field to the secondary coil","marks":1},{"text":"the changing field induces an (alternating) e.m.f. in the secondary","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Finding the output voltage). Start like this: Formula: ⟪V_{s}=V_{p}\\times N_{s}/N_{p}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week11.11.4.p4","kind":"self","q":"Why is the core of a transformer made of **soft** iron?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised (1), so it follows the alternating field / concentrates and links the field to the secondary with little energy loss (1).","points":[{"text":"Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised","marks":1},{"text":"so it follows the alternating field / concentrates and links the field to the secondary with little energy loss","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week11.11.4.p5","kind":"self","q":"A transformer reduces 240 V to 12 V. The primary has 1200 turns. Calculate the number of turns on the secondary.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪N_{s}=1200\\times 12/240⟫ (1) ⟪=60⟫ turns (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪N_{s}=1200\\times 12/240⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=60⟫ turns","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Finding the number of turns). Start like this: Formula: ⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}/V_{p}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.4.p6","kind":"self","q":"A transformer has 200 primary turns and 1000 secondary turns. The input is 12 V a.c. Calculate the output voltage and state the type of transformer.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪V_{s}=12\\times 1000/200⟫ (1) ⟪=60\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫ (1); step-up (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪V_{s}=12\\times 1000/200⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=60\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"step-up","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Finding the output voltage). Start like this: Formula: ⟪V_{s}=V_{p}\\times N_{s}/N_{p}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week11.11.4.p7","kind":"num","q":"An ideal transformer has an input of 240 V. Its output is 24 V and the secondary current is 5.0 A. Calculate the primary current.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}/V_{p}=24\\times 5.0/240⟫ (1) ⟪=0.50\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}/V_{p}=24\\times 5.0/240⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.50\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.5,"sf":2,"unit":"A","unitTex":"\\mathrm{A}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Finding the output voltage). Start like this: Formula: ⟪V_{s}=V_{p}\\times N_{s}/N_{p}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week11.11.4.p8","kind":"self","q":"A doorbell works from an 8.0 V supply provided by a transformer connected to 240 V mains. The primary has 3000 turns. (a) Calculate the number of secondary turns. (b) The bell takes 0.60 A. Assuming the transformer is ideal, calculate the primary current.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"(a) ⟪N_{s}=3000\\times 8.0/240⟫ (1) ⟪=100⟫ turns (1). (b) ⟪I_{p}=8.0\\times 0.60/240⟫ (1) ⟪=0.020\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) ⟪N_{s}=3000\\times 8.0/240⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=100⟫ turns","marks":1},{"text":"(b) ⟪I_{p}=8.0\\times 0.60/240⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.020\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 4 (Efficiency of a real transformer). Start like this: Input power: ⟪V_{p}I_{p}=240\\times 0.50=120\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫","from":4}}],"exit":[{"id":"week11.11.4.p9","kind":"self","q":"A transformer takes 2.0 A from a 240 V supply and delivers 20 A at 22 V. Calculate its efficiency and state where the wasted energy goes.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Input ⟪=240\\times 2.0=480\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫ (1); output ⟪=22\\times 20=440\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫ (1); efficiency ⟪=440/480\\times 100=92\\%\\ (91.7\\%)⟫ (1); wasted as heat in the coils/core (1).","points":[{"text":"Input ⟪=240\\times 2.0=480\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"output ⟪=22\\times 20=440\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"efficiency ⟪=440/480\\times 100=92\\%\\ (91.7\\%)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"wasted as heat in the coils/core","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week11.11.4.p10","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the core of a transformer is laminated.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The changing field induces eddy currents in the core, which heat it and waste energy (1); thin insulated layers (laminations) reduce/block the eddy currents, so less energy is lost (1).","points":[{"text":"The changing field induces eddy currents in the core, which heat it and waste energy","marks":1},{"text":"thin insulated layers (laminations) reduce/block the eddy currents, so less energy is lost","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week11.11.4.w1","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the output of the generator is alternating.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Every half turn, each side of the coil changes from moving up to moving down through the field (1), so the direction of the induced e.m.f./current reverses (1).","points":[{"text":"Every half turn, each side of the coil changes from moving up to moving down through the field","marks":1},{"text":"so the direction of the induced e.m.f./current reverses","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.3.p4"},{"id":"week11.11.4.w2","kind":"self","q":"State the energy change that takes place in a generator.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Kinetic (mechanical) energy → electrical energy (1).","points":[{"text":"Kinetic (mechanical) energy → electrical energy","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.3.p1"},{"id":"week11.11.4.w3","kind":"self","q":"State the function of (a) the slip rings, (b) the carbon brushes in an a.c. generator.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) Keep each end of the rotating coil connected to the same brush/outside circuit without twisting the wires (1). (b) Make sliding contact with the slip rings to carry current to the external circuit (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Keep each end of the rotating coil connected to the same brush/outside circuit without twisting the wires","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Make sliding contact with the slip rings to carry current to the external circuit","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.3.p2"}],"n":89},{"id":"week11.11.5","kind":"unit","unit":"11.5","title":"Sending Electricity Across the Country: Why a.c. and High Voltage?","titleText":"Sending Electricity Across the Country: Why a.c. and High Voltage?","goals":[{"code":"D7.14","text":"Discuss the advantages of using a.c. for the transmission of electrical energy"}],"codes":["D7.14"],"words":[{"term":"Generator","def":"A device that changes kinetic energy into electrical energy by electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Slip rings","def":"Two complete rings joined to the ends of a generator coil, keeping it connected to the external circuit (a.c. output)."},{"term":"Transformer","def":"A device that steps an alternating voltage up or down using electromagnetic induction."},{"term":"Step-up transformer","def":"More turns on the secondary than the primary; output voltage greater than input."},{"term":"Step-down transformer","def":"Fewer turns on the secondary than the primary; output voltage less than input."},{"term":"Ideal transformer","def":"A 100% efficient transformer: input power = output power."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Power stations are often far from the towns that use the electricity. The energy is carried along **transmission lines** (thick aluminium cables on tall pylons). This network is called the **grid**."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/transmission","alt":"Figure 11.10 — Transmission of electrical energy from the power station to consumers","caption":"Figure 11.10 — Transmission of electrical energy from the power station to consumers","width":6,"w":1400,"h":416},{"type":"h","text":"The problem: cables get hot"},{"type":"p","text":"The cables are long, so they have **resistance**. Current flowing through a resistance heats it. The power wasted as heat in the cables is:"},{"type":"formula","tex":"P_{\\text{loss}}=I^{2}R","where":["P~loss~ = power wasted as heat in the cables (W)","⟪I⟫ = current in the cables (A);  R = total resistance of the cables (Ω) — remember to count BOTH wires (there and back)","Because of the **square**, halving the current makes the loss FOUR times smaller."],"label":"POWER LOST IN TRANSMISSION LINES"},{"type":"h","text":"The solution: high voltage, small current"},{"type":"p","text":"The power sent is ⟪P=IV⟫. For the SAME power, if the voltage is made **bigger**, the current is **smaller** ⟪(I=P\\div V)⟫. A smaller current means much less I^2^R heating."},{"type":"table","headers":["Voltage increased by…","Current becomes…","Power loss (I^2^R) becomes…"],"rows":[["× 2","÷ 2","÷ 4"],["× 10","÷ 10","÷ 100"],["× 20","÷ 20","÷ 400"]]},{"type":"steps","items":["At the power station, the generator voltage (about 11–25 kV) is increased by a **step-up transformer** to a very high voltage (e.g. 132 kV).","The energy travels through the transmission lines at high voltage and **small current**, so very little energy is wasted as heat.","Near towns, **step-down transformers** in substations reduce the voltage in stages.","Homes receive a **safe** low voltage (110–240 V, depending on the country)."]},{"type":"h","text":"Advantages of using a.c. for transmission (D7.14)"},{"type":"bullets","items":["a.c. voltages can be **stepped up and down easily and efficiently using transformers**. Transformers do NOT work with steady d.c.","So a.c. can be sent at **high voltage → small current → much less energy lost as heat (I^2^R)** in the cables.","Smaller current means **thinner, lighter, cheaper cables** and fewer/lighter pylons can be used.","The voltage can be **stepped down** to a safe value for homes and to different values for factories.","a.c. generators (with slip rings) are simpler and more reliable than d.c. generators (commutators wear out)."]},{"type":"tip","text":"The key chain for 3–4 marks: **a.c. → transformers can step up voltage → high voltage means low current **⟪\\boldsymbol{(P=IV)}⟫** → low current means low I^2^R heat loss in cables.** Write every link."},{"type":"warn","text":"Saying \"high voltage means less energy loss because voltage is lost in the cables\". The loss depends on the CURRENT: ⟪P_{\\text{loss}}=I^{2}R⟫. High voltage only helps because it makes the current SMALL."},{"type":"p","text":"**Disadvantages of high-voltage lines:** very dangerous (can arc through air); need tall pylons and large insulators; expensive transformers at each end."}],"examples":[{"id":"week11.11.5.e1","title":"Low voltage vs high voltage transmission","question":"A power station sends 100 kW through cables of total resistance 5.0 Ω. Calculate the power lost in the cables if the power is sent at (a) 1000 V; (b) 20 000 V.","steps":[{"label":"(a) Current","text":"⟪I=P/V=100\\,000/1000=100\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫"},{"label":"(a) Loss","text":"⟪P_{\\text{loss}}=I^{2}R=100^{2}\\times 5.0=50\\,000\\,\\mathrm{W}=50\\,\\mathrm{kW}⟫ (HALF the power wasted!)"},{"label":"(b) Current","text":"⟪I=100\\,000/20\\,000=5.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Loss","text":"⟪P_{\\text{loss}}=5.0^{2}\\times 5.0=125\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫ (only 0.125%)"}],"answer":"(a) 50 kW  (b) 125 W — twenty times the voltage gives 400 times less loss"},{"id":"week11.11.5.e2","title":"Step-up transformer at a power station","question":"A generator produces 500 kW at 25 kV. A step-up transformer raises this to 250 kV. The transmission line has resistance 40 Ω. Find (a) the turns ratio N~s~ : N~p~; (b) the current in the line; (c) the power lost in the line. (Assume the transformer is ideal.)","steps":[{"label":"(a) Turns ratio","text":"⟪N_{s}/N_{p}=V_{s}/V_{p}=250/25=10⟫, so 10 : 1"},{"label":"(b) Current","text":"⟪I=P/V=500\\,000/250\\,000=2.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫"},{"label":"(c) Loss","text":"⟪P_{\\text{loss}}=I^{2}R=2.0^{2}\\times 40=160\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫"}],"answer":"(a) 10 : 1  (b) 2.0 A  (c) 160 W"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week11.11.5.p1","kind":"self","q":"Write down the formula for the power wasted as heat in a transmission cable, naming each symbol.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪P=I^{2}R⟫ (1); I = current in the cable, R = resistance of the cable (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪P=I^{2}R⟫","marks":1},{"text":"I = current in the cable, R = resistance of the cable","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week11.11.5.p2","kind":"self","q":"Explain why electrical energy is transmitted at a very high voltage.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"For the same power, ⟪P=IV⟫, so a high voltage means a small current (1); power loss in the cables ⟪=I^{2}R⟫ (1), so a small current means much less energy wasted as heat (1).","points":[{"text":"For the same power, ⟪P=IV⟫, so a high voltage means a small current","marks":1},{"text":"power loss in the cables ⟪=I^{2}R⟫","marks":1},{"text":"so a small current means much less energy wasted as heat","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Low voltage vs high voltage transmission). Start like this: (a) Current: ⟪I=P/V=100\\,000/1000=100\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week11.11.5.p3","kind":"self","q":"State TWO advantages of using a.c. rather than d.c. for transmitting electrical energy.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two (1 each): a.c. voltage can be stepped up/down easily with transformers; so it can be transmitted at high voltage/low current with low I^2^R losses; thinner/cheaper cables; can be stepped down to a safe voltage for homes.","points":[{"text":"Any two (1 each): a.c. voltage can be stepped up/down easily with transformers; so it can be transmitted at high voltage/low current with low I^2^R losses; thinner/cheaper cables; can be stepped down to a safe voltage for homes.","marks":2,"each":true}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week11.11.5.p4","kind":"self","q":"50 kW is sent at 250 V through cables of total resistance 0.40 Ω. Calculate (a) the current; (b) the power lost in the cables.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(a) ⟪I=50\\,000/250=200\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1). (b) ⟪P=200^{2}\\times 0.40⟫ (1) ⟪=16\\,000\\,\\mathrm{W}=16\\,\\mathrm{kW}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) ⟪I=50\\,000/250=200\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(b) ⟪P=200^{2}\\times 0.40⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=16\\,000\\,\\mathrm{W}=16\\,\\mathrm{kW}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Low voltage vs high voltage transmission). Start like this: (a) Current: ⟪I=P/V=100\\,000/1000=100\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.5.p5","kind":"num","q":"The same 50 kW is now sent at 10 000 V through the same cables. Calculate the new power loss.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪I=50\\,000/10\\,000=5.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1); ⟪P=5.0^{2}\\times 0.40⟫ (1) ⟪=10\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪I=50\\,000/10\\,000=5.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪P=5.0^{2}\\times 0.40⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=10\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":10,"sf":1,"unit":"W","unitTex":"\\mathrm{W}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Low voltage vs high voltage transmission). Start like this: (a) Current: ⟪I=P/V=100\\,000/1000=100\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.5.p6","kind":"self","q":"The transmission voltage is increased by a factor of 10 for the same power. State what happens to (a) the current, (b) the power lost in the cables.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) current ÷ 10 (1) (b) power loss ÷ 100 (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) current ÷ 10","marks":1},{"text":"(b) power loss ÷ 100","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Low voltage vs high voltage transmission). Start like this: (a) Current: ⟪I=P/V=100\\,000/1000=100\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week11.11.5.p7","kind":"self","q":"Why is the voltage stepped down before it reaches homes?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Very high voltages are dangerous / for safety; appliances are designed for 110–240 V (1).","points":[{"text":"Very high voltages are dangerous / for safety; appliances are designed for 110–240 V","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week11.11.5.p8","kind":"self","q":"A power station generator gives 11 kV at 200 A. An ideal transformer steps it up to 132 kV. Calculate (a) the turns ratio N~s~ : N~p~; (b) the current in the transmission line.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"(a) ⟪N_{s}/N_{p}=132/11⟫ (1) ⟪=12⟫, i.e. 12 : 1 (1). (b) ⟪I_{s}=V_{p}I_{p}/V_{s}=11\\,000\\times 200/132\\,000⟫ (1) ⟪=17\\,\\mathrm{A}\\ (16.7\\,\\mathrm{A})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) ⟪N_{s}/N_{p}=132/11⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=12⟫, i.e. 12 : 1","marks":1},{"text":"(b) ⟪I_{s}=V_{p}I_{p}/V_{s}=11\\,000\\times 200/132\\,000⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=17\\,\\mathrm{A}\\ (16.7\\,\\mathrm{A})⟫","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week11.11.5.p9","kind":"self","q":"State ONE disadvantage of transmitting electricity at very high voltage.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any one (1): dangerous/risk of electrocution or arcing; needs tall pylons and large insulators; expensive transformers needed.","points":[{"text":"Any one. : dangerous/risk of electrocution or arcing; needs tall pylons and large insulators; expensive transformers needed","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week11.11.5.w1","kind":"num","q":"An ideal transformer has an input of 240 V. Its output is 24 V and the secondary current is 5.0 A. Calculate the primary current.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}/V_{p}=24\\times 5.0/240⟫ (1) ⟪=0.50\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}/V_{p}=24\\times 5.0/240⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=0.50\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.5,"sf":2,"unit":"A","unitTex":"\\mathrm{A}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.4.p7"},{"id":"week11.11.5.w2","kind":"self","q":"Why is the core of a transformer made of **soft** iron?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised (1), so it follows the alternating field / concentrates and links the field to the secondary with little energy loss (1).","points":[{"text":"Soft iron is easily magnetised and demagnetised","marks":1},{"text":"so it follows the alternating field / concentrates and links the field to the secondary with little energy loss","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.4.p4"},{"id":"week11.11.5.w3","kind":"self","q":"A transformer reduces 240 V to 12 V. The primary has 1200 turns. Calculate the number of turns on the secondary.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪N_{s}=1200\\times 12/240⟫ (1) ⟪=60⟫ turns (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪N_{s}=1200\\times 12/240⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=60⟫ turns","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week11.11.4.p5"}],"n":90},{"id":"week11.check","kind":"check","title":"Book check","titleText":"Book check","check":true,"watchOut":["Saying a magnet held STILL in a coil induces a current. Only MOVEMENT (change) induces an e.m.f.","Using the LEFT hand for a generator. Generators (induced current) = RIGHT hand; motors (force) = LEFT hand.","Getting Lenz's law backwards: the coil OPPOSES the change — it repels an approaching pole and attracts a departing pole.","Saying the generator e.m.f. is biggest when the coil is vertical (facing the poles). It is ZERO there; maximum when the coil is horizontal.","Calling slip rings a commutator, or saying slip rings reverse the current. Slip rings → a.c.; split-ring commutator → motor/d.c.","Turning the transformer ratio upside down. Check: step-down must give a SMALLER answer for V~s~; step-up a BIGGER one.","Thinking a step-up transformer increases power. Voltage up → current DOWN; ideal power in = power out.","Explaining high-voltage transmission without mentioning CURRENT. The chain is: high V → low I → low I^2^R loss.","Forgetting to square the current in ⟪P=I^{2}R⟫, or forgetting to convert kW to W and kV to V before dividing.","Saying a transformer works on d.c. A steady current makes a steady field: no change, no output."],"mcq":[{"id":"week11.check.m1","kind":"mcq","q":"A bar magnet is held stationary inside a coil connected to a galvanometer. The galvanometer reads zero because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["no magnetic field lines are being cut","the magnet is too weak","the coil has too few turns","the current is too large to measure"],"answer":"A","explanation":"An e.m.f. is induced only when field lines are cut (the field is changing). B and C would give a smaller reading, not zero, if the magnet were moving.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m2","kind":"mcq","q":"Which of the following would NOT increase the e.m.f. induced when a magnet is pushed into a coil?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["pushing the magnet in faster","using a coil with more turns","using a stronger magnet","using thicker wire for the coil"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Thicker wire lowers resistance but does not change the rate of cutting field lines, so the e.m.f. is unchanged. A, B and C all increase the rate of cutting (Faraday's law).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m3","kind":"mcq","q":"Lenz's law is a consequence of the principle of conservation of","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["energy","mass","charge","momentum"],"answer":"A","explanation":"The induced current opposes the motion, so work must be done; that work becomes electrical energy. Charge (C) is conserved too, but it is not what Lenz's law expresses.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m4","kind":"mcq","q":"The N pole of a magnet is moved TOWARDS the end X of a coil. End X becomes","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["a N pole, which attracts the magnet","a N pole, which repels the magnet","a S pole, which attracts the magnet","a S pole, which repels the magnet"],"answer":"B","explanation":"By Lenz's law, the coil opposes the approach, so X becomes N and repels. C would help the motion, breaking conservation of energy. D is impossible: S attracts N.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m5","kind":"mcq","q":"In Fleming's right-hand rule, the second finger points in the direction of the","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["motion of the conductor","magnetic field","induced current","force on the conductor"],"answer":"C","explanation":"seCond finger = Current. Thumb = Motion, First finger = Field. 'Force' (D) belongs to the left-hand (motor) rule.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m6","kind":"mcq","q":"In a simple a.c. generator, the slip rings","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["connect the rotating coil to the external circuit","reverse the current every half turn","increase the strength of the magnetic field","change a.c. into d.c."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Slip rings (with brushes) keep each end of the coil connected to the outside circuit. B and D describe a split-ring commutator.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m7","kind":"mcq","q":"The e.m.f. of a simple generator is MAXIMUM when the plane of the coil is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["perpendicular to the magnetic field","parallel to the magnetic field","at 45° to the magnetic field","not moving"],"answer":"B","explanation":"When the coil's plane is parallel to the field, its sides move at right angles to the field lines and cut them fastest. When perpendicular (A), the sides move along the lines, so ⟪e=0⟫.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m8","kind":"mcq","q":"One complete cycle of an alternating voltage takes 0.02 s. Its frequency is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.02 Hz","20 Hz","50 Hz","500 Hz"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪f=1/T=1/0.02=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫. A just repeats the period.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m9","kind":"mcq","q":"The coil of a generator is turned twice as fast. Which row shows the effect on the peak e.m.f. and the frequency?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["peak doubles, frequency unchanged","peak unchanged, frequency doubles","peak doubles, frequency doubles","peak halves, frequency doubles"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Faster rotation means field lines are cut twice as fast (peak doubles) AND twice as many cycles per second (frequency doubles). A forgets the frequency change.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m10","kind":"mcq","q":"A transformer will NOT work with a d.c. supply because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["d.c. is too dangerous","d.c. produces no magnetic field","d.c. produces a magnetic field that does not change","the core would melt"],"answer":"C","explanation":"d.c. does make a magnetic field (so B is wrong), but it is steady; only a CHANGING field induces an e.m.f. in the secondary.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m11","kind":"mcq","q":"A transformer has 100 turns on the primary and 500 turns on the secondary. The input is 10 V a.c. The output voltage is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["2 V","10 V","50 V","5000 V"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪V_{s}=V_{p}\\times N_{s}/N_{p}=10\\times 500/100=50\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫. A uses the ratio upside down.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m12","kind":"mcq","q":"An ideal transformer has ⟪V_{p}=240\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫ and ⟪I_{p}=0.50\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫. The output voltage is 12 V. The output current is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.025 A","0.50 A","6.0 A","10 A"],"answer":"D","explanation":"⟪V_{p}I_{p}=V_{s}I_{s}\\rightarrow I_{s}=240\\times 0.50/12=10\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫. Step-down in voltage means step-up in current; A divides the wrong way.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m13","kind":"mcq","q":"The core of a transformer is laminated in order to","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["increase the output voltage","reduce eddy currents","make the core lighter to carry","increase the number of turns"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Thin insulated layers block the eddy currents induced in the core, reducing heat loss. Lamination does not change the voltage (A).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m14","kind":"mcq","q":"Electrical energy is transmitted at high voltage mainly to","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["make the electricity travel faster","reduce the current and so reduce the I^2^R loss in the cables","increase the power delivered by the station","make it safer for people living near the lines"],"answer":"B","explanation":"High V → low I for the same power ⟪(P=IV)⟫ → much smaller I^2^R heating. D is wrong: high voltage is MORE dangerous.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week11.check.m15","kind":"mcq","q":"The current in a transmission line is halved while the resistance stays the same. The power lost as heat in the line becomes","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["twice as large","half as large","one quarter as large","unchanged"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪P=I^{2}R⟫: ⟪(\\tfrac{1}{2})^{2}=\\tfrac{1}{4}⟫. B forgets to square the current.","section":"p01"}],"structured":[{"id":"week11.check.s1","kind":"multi","title":"Electromagnetic induction and Lenz's law","total":12,"stem":["A student connects a coil to a centre-zero galvanometer. She pushes the N pole of a bar magnet into the coil and the needle moves to the right."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/magnet_coil","alt":"Figure S1","caption":"Figure S1","width":3.8,"w":1063,"h":654}],"parts":[{"id":"week11.check.s1.a","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the needle moves when the magnet is pushed into the coil.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The coil cuts magnetic field lines / the magnetic field in the coil changes (1), so an e.m.f. (current) is induced (1).","points":[{"text":"The coil cuts magnetic field lines / the magnetic field in the coil changes","marks":1},{"text":"so an e.m.f. (current) is induced","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week11.check.s1.b","kind":"self","q":"State what the needle does when (i) the magnet is held still inside the coil; (ii) the magnet is pulled out of the coil.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(i) reads zero / returns to zero (1). (ii) moves to the LEFT (opposite direction) (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) reads zero / returns to zero","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) moves to the LEFT (opposite direction)","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week11.check.s1.c","kind":"self","q":"State TWO changes that would make the needle move further.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Any two (1 each): push the magnet faster; stronger magnet; more turns on the coil; soft-iron core.","points":[{"text":"Any two (1 each): push the magnet faster; stronger magnet; more turns on the coil; soft-iron core.","marks":2,"each":true}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week11.check.s1.d","kind":"self","q":"State Lenz's law.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The induced current flows in a direction (1) that opposes the change/motion producing it (1).","points":[{"text":"The induced current flows in a direction","marks":1},{"text":"that opposes the change/motion producing it","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week11.check.s1.e","kind":"self","q":"State the magnetic pole produced at the end of the coil nearest the magnet as the magnet is pushed in, and explain, in terms of energy, why it must be this pole.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"North pole (1). It repels the magnet, so work must be done to push the magnet in (1); this work is converted into electrical energy — if it attracted, energy would be created from nothing (conservation of energy) (1).","points":[{"text":"North pole","marks":1},{"text":"It repels the magnet, so work must be done to push the magnet in","marks":1},{"text":"this work is converted into electrical energy — if it attracted, energy would be created from nothing (conservation of energy)","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week11.check.s1.f","kind":"self","q":"Name the rule used to find the direction of the current induced in a straight wire moving through a magnetic field.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Fleming's right-hand rule (1).","points":[{"text":"Fleming's right-hand rule","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"}]},{"id":"week11.check.s2","kind":"multi","title":"A transformer for a 12 V lamp","total":13,"stem":["A transformer is used to light a 12 V, 36 W lamp from the 240 V a.c. mains. The primary coil has 2000 turns."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week11.check.s2.a","kind":"self","q":"Draw a labelled diagram of a simple transformer. Label the primary coil, the secondary coil and the core.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"Closed (rectangular) iron core with two separate coils wound on it (1); primary labelled, connected to a.c. input (1); secondary labelled, connected to output/lamp; core labelled (laminated soft iron) (1).","points":[{"text":"Closed (rectangular) iron core with two separate coils wound on it","marks":1},{"text":"primary labelled, connected to a.c. input","marks":1},{"text":"secondary labelled, connected to output/lamp; core labelled (laminated soft iron)","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week11.check.s2.b","kind":"self","q":"Explain how the transformer produces a voltage across the lamp.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"Alternating current in the primary (1) produces a changing magnetic field in the core (1); the core links the changing field with the secondary coil (1); an alternating e.m.f. is induced in the secondary (1).","points":[{"text":"Alternating current in the primary","marks":1},{"text":"produces a changing magnetic field in the core","marks":1},{"text":"the core links the changing field with the secondary coil","marks":1},{"text":"an alternating e.m.f. is induced in the secondary","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week11.check.s2.c","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the number of turns on the secondary coil.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}/V_{p}=2000\\times 12/240⟫ (1) ⟪=100⟫ turns (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}/V_{p}=2000\\times 12/240⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=100⟫ turns","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week11.check.s2.d","kind":"self","q":"Assuming the transformer is ideal, calculate (i) the current in the lamp; (ii) the current in the primary coil.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"(i) ⟪I_{s}=P/V=36/12=3.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1). (ii) ⟪I_{p}=36/240=0.15\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪I_{s}=P/V=36/12=3.0\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) ⟪I_{p}=36/240=0.15\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week11.check.s2.e","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the core is laminated.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Changing field induces eddy currents in the core which waste energy as heat (1); insulated laminations reduce eddy currents (1).","points":[{"text":"Changing field induces eddy currents in the core which waste energy as heat","marks":1},{"text":"insulated laminations reduce eddy currents","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"}]},{"id":"week11.check.s3","kind":"multi","title":"Generating and transmitting electricity","total":12,"stem":["Figure S3 shows the output of a simple a.c. generator (solid line)."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"img","src":"img/week11/generator_faster","alt":"Figure S3","caption":"Figure S3","width":4.4,"w":1174,"h":574}],"parts":[{"id":"week11.check.s3.a","kind":"self","q":"Using the SOLID line, state the peak voltage and the period, and calculate the frequency.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Peak ⟪=6\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫ (1); period ⟪=40\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.040\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1); ⟪f=1/0.040=25\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Peak ⟪=6\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"period ⟪=40\\,\\mathrm{ms}=0.040\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪f=1/0.040=25\\,\\mathrm{Hz}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week11.check.s3.b","kind":"self","q":"The dashed line shows the output after one change was made. State the change and explain the new shape.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The coil was turned twice as fast (1); field lines are cut twice as fast so peak doubles (12 V) and there are twice as many cycles per second (frequency doubles, 50 Hz) (1).","points":[{"text":"The coil was turned twice as fast","marks":1},{"text":"field lines are cut twice as fast so peak doubles (12 V) and there are twice as many cycles per second (frequency doubles, 50 Hz)","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week11.check.s3.c","kind":"self","q":"Explain why electrical energy is transmitted across the country at very high voltage.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"For the same power, ⟪P=IV⟫, so high voltage gives small current (1); energy lost as heat in cables ⟪=I^{2}R⟫ (1); small current → much less energy wasted (1).","points":[{"text":"For the same power, ⟪P=IV⟫, so high voltage gives small current","marks":1},{"text":"energy lost as heat in cables ⟪=I^{2}R⟫","marks":1},{"text":"small current → much less energy wasted","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week11.check.s3.d","kind":"self","q":"A power station sends 200 kW through cables of total resistance 2.0 Ω. Calculate the power lost in the cables when it is sent at (i) 2000 V; (ii) 20 000 V.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"(i) ⟪I=200\\,000/2000=100\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1); loss ⟪=100^{2}\\times 2.0=20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{W}\\ (20\\,\\mathrm{kW})⟫ (1). (ii) ⟪I=10\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1); loss ⟪=10^{2}\\times 2.0=200\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪I=200\\,000/2000=100\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"loss ⟪=100^{2}\\times 2.0=20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{W}\\ (20\\,\\mathrm{kW})⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) ⟪I=10\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"loss ⟪=10^{2}\\times 2.0=200\\,\\mathrm{W}⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"}]}],"n":91},{"id":"week11.past","kind":"past","title":"Past-paper practice","titleText":"Past-paper practice","check":true,"log":true,"questions":[{"id":"week11.past.q1","kind":"multi","title":"Data analysis (Paper 02 Question 1 style)","total":25,"stem":["A student investigated how the output voltage, V~s~, of a transformer depends on the number of turns, N~s~, on the secondary coil. The primary coil had 400 turns and was connected to a 12.0 V a.c. supply. The results are shown below."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Number of secondary turns, N~s~","50","100","150","200","250","300","350"],"rows":[["Output voltage, V~s~ / V","1.4","2.9","4.4","5.8","7.2","8.6","10.1"]]}],"parts":[{"id":"week11.past.q1.a","kind":"self","q":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of V~s~ (y-axis) against N~s~ (x-axis). Draw the line of best fit.","marks":7,"level":3,"img":{"src":"img/week11/graph_paper","alt":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of Vs (y-axis) against Ns (x-axis). Draw the line of best fit.","w":1094,"h":894},"lines":0,"scheme":"Axes labelled with quantity and unit: V~s~ / V and ⟪N_{s}\\ (1+1)⟫; suitable scale using more than half the grid (e.g. 50 turns and 1 V per big square) (1); all 7 points plotted correctly (2: −1 per error); ruled straight best-fit line through (or very near) the origin (1); title (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes labelled with quantity and unit: V~s~ / V and N~s~","marks":2},{"text":"suitable scale using more than half the grid (e.g. 50 turns and 1 V per big square)","marks":1},{"text":"all 7 points plotted correctly","marks":2},{"text":"ruled straight best-fit line through (or very near) the origin","marks":1},{"text":"title","marks":1}],"label":"(a)","grid":true},{"id":"week11.past.q1.b","kind":"self","q":"Determine the gradient of your graph. Show clearly the triangle used.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Large triangle shown on the line (1); correct ΔV~s~ and ΔN~s~ read from the line (1); gradient ≈ 0.029 V per turn (accept 0.028–0.030) (1); unit V per turn / V turn^−1^ (1).","points":[{"text":"Large triangle shown on the line","marks":1},{"text":"correct ΔV~s~ and ΔN~s~ read from the line","marks":1},{"text":"gradient ≈ 0.029 V per turn (accept 0.028–0.030)","marks":1},{"text":"unit V per turn / V turn^−1^","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week11.past.q1.c","kind":"self","q":"For an ideal transformer, gradient ⟪=V_{p}/N_{p}⟫. Calculate this theoretical value and compare it with your gradient.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪12.0/400=0.030\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫ per turn (1); measured gradient is slightly smaller (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪12.0/400=0.030\\,\\mathrm{V}⟫ per turn","marks":1},{"text":"measured gradient is slightly smaller","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week11.past.q1.d","kind":"self","q":"Suggest why the student's values of V~s~ are slightly less than the values predicted for an ideal transformer.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The transformer is not 100% efficient / energy is lost (1), e.g. some field lines do not link the secondary (flux leakage), heating of coils (resistance), eddy currents in the core (1).","points":[{"text":"The transformer is not 100% efficient / energy is lost","marks":1},{"text":"e.g. some field lines do not link the secondary (flux leakage), heating of coils (resistance), eddy currents in the core","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week11.past.q1.e","kind":"self","q":"Use your graph to find the number of secondary turns needed to give an output of 6.0 V. Show on the graph how you obtained your answer.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Dashed lines drawn from 6.0 V to the line and down to the N~s~ axis (1); ⟪N_{s}\\approx 210⟫ turns (accept 200–215) (1).","points":[{"text":"Dashed lines drawn from 6.0 V to the line and down to the N~s~ axis","marks":1},{"text":"⟪N_{s}\\approx 210⟫ turns (accept 200–215)","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week11.past.q1.f","kind":"self","q":"When ⟪N_{s}=350⟫, is the transformer a step-up or a step-down transformer? Give a reason.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Step-down (1); N~s~ (350) is less than N~p~ (400), so V~s~ < V~p~ (1).","points":[{"text":"Step-down","marks":1},{"text":"N~s~ (350) is less than N~p~ (400), so V~s~ < V~p~","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"},{"id":"week11.past.q1.g","kind":"self","q":"Using the ideal transformer equation, calculate the number of secondary turns needed to give 24 V from this primary coil.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}/V_{p}=400\\times 24/12.0⟫ (1) ⟪=800⟫ turns (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪N_{s}=N_{p}\\times V_{s}/V_{p}=400\\times 24/12.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=800⟫ turns","marks":1}],"label":"(g)"},{"id":"week11.past.q1.h","kind":"self","q":"Explain why the primary coil must be connected to an a.c. supply and not to a battery.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"a.c. produces a changing magnetic field in the core (1); only a changing field induces an e.m.f. in the secondary — a battery's steady d.c. gives no output (1).","points":[{"text":"a.c. produces a changing magnetic field in the core","marks":1},{"text":"only a changing field induces an e.m.f. in the secondary — a battery's steady d.c. gives no output","marks":1}],"label":"(h)"},{"id":"week11.past.q1.i","kind":"self","q":"State TWO precautions the student should take to obtain reliable results.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two (1 each): keep the input voltage constant/check it each time; use the same core and primary coil throughout; check the voltmeter zero / avoid parallax; repeat readings and average; ensure good connections.","points":[{"text":"Any two (1 each): keep the input voltage constant/check it each time; use the same core and primary coil throughout; check the voltmeter zero / avoid parallax; repeat readings and average; ensure good connections.","marks":2,"each":true}],"label":"(i)"}]},{"id":"week11.past.q2","kind":"multi","title":"The generator and the grid (extended response)","total":15,"stem":["Most of the electrical energy used in the Caribbean is produced by a.c. generators in power stations and carried to consumers by transmission lines."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week11.past.q2.a","kind":"self","q":"Describe the structure of a simple a.c. generator. You may use a labelled diagram.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"Coil (of many turns) on an axle that can rotate (1); between the poles of a magnet (N and S) (1); ends of the coil connected to two slip rings, with carbon brushes connecting to the external circuit (1).","points":[{"text":"Coil (of many turns) on an axle that can rotate","marks":1},{"text":"between the poles of a magnet (N and S)","marks":1},{"text":"ends of the coil connected to two slip rings, with carbon brushes connecting to the external circuit","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week11.past.q2.b","kind":"self","q":"Explain how the generator produces an ALTERNATING e.m.f.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"The coil is rotated so its sides cut magnetic field lines (1); an e.m.f. is induced in the coil (electromagnetic induction / Faraday) (1); every half turn each side changes from moving up to moving down through the field (1), so the direction of the induced e.m.f. reverses — alternating (1).","points":[{"text":"The coil is rotated so its sides cut magnetic field lines","marks":1},{"text":"an e.m.f. is induced in the coil (electromagnetic induction / Faraday)","marks":1},{"text":"every half turn each side changes from moving up to moving down through the field","marks":1},{"text":"so the direction of the induced e.m.f. reverses — alternating","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week11.past.q2.c","kind":"self","q":"Sketch a graph of e.m.f. against time for ONE revolution, starting with the coil vertical. Mark on your sketch a point where the coil is horizontal.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"Sine curve starting at zero (1); one complete cycle with equal positive and negative peaks (1); a peak (or trough) marked as coil horizontal (1).","points":[{"text":"Sine curve starting at zero","marks":1},{"text":"one complete cycle with equal positive and negative peaks","marks":1},{"text":"a peak (or trough) marked as coil horizontal","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week11.past.q2.d","kind":"self","q":"A power station generates 5.0 MW at 25 kV. The voltage is stepped up to 250 kV before transmission through lines of total resistance 8.0 Ω. Assuming the transformer is ideal, calculate (i) the current in the lines; (ii) the power lost in the lines.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"(i) ⟪I=P/V=5\\,000\\,000/250\\,000⟫ (1) ⟪=20\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫ (1). (ii) ⟪P=I^{2}R=20^{2}\\times 8.0⟫ (1) ⟪=3200\\,\\mathrm{W}\\ (3.2\\,\\mathrm{kW})⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(i) ⟪I=P/V=5\\,000\\,000/250\\,000⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=20\\,\\mathrm{A}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(ii) ⟪P=I^{2}R=20^{2}\\times 8.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=3200\\,\\mathrm{W}\\ (3.2\\,\\mathrm{kW})⟫","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week11.past.q2.e","kind":"self","q":"Why is the voltage stepped down before it reaches homes?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"High voltage is dangerous — reduced to a safe value (110–240 V) for appliances (1).","points":[{"text":"High voltage is dangerous — reduced to a safe value (110–240 V) for appliances","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"}]}],"n":92}]};
